---
.title = "Capability networking and durable state in Zig",
.description = "OCapN and CapTP networking with Loam transactional storage and durable actors. A low-level Zig library, Apache-2.0, 0.1.1 alpha.",
.date = .date("2026-06-12T00:00:00"),
.layout = "index.shtml",
.draft = false,
---

glowcap is a low-level [Zig](https://ziglang.org/) library for capability-based
communication and durable application state. It combines
[OCapN](https://github.com/ocapn/ocapn) and CapTP networking with
[Loam]($link.page('loam')), its transactional storage and durable actor system.
Applications control memory, I/O, and scheduling.

**0.1.1 alpha · Apache-2.0.** APIs, ABIs, and storage formats can change before
1.0. Read [Project & status]($link.page('project')) before depending on it.

Peers exchange references to objects and call them across a network. Loam
stores the objects' state and relationships, wakes actors on demand, and
provides durable names peers can use after a restart. The design targets low
overhead, with explicit ownership and checked allocation and copy budgets.
[Performance]($link.page('performance')) reports measurements and their limits.

glowcap is built with AI coding assistants. The maintainer directs the work
and is answerable for what lands.
[PROVENANCE.md](https://github.com/glowcap-org/glowcap/blob/main/PROVENANCE.md)
records how.

If capabilities are new to you, [Why OCapN]($link.page('why')) compares them
with the bearer tokens and login tables you already use.
[What about identity?]($link.page('identity')) covers where people, bots,
and AI agents fit, and what keys can and cannot answer.

[Their program hands yours a reference to an object called "greeter." Holding that reference is what lets you call it.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-capability.svg'))

[Rooms]($link.page('rooms')) is a chat app built on this idea. Pick a member in a
room and press **Message *name* directly**, and your messages go straight to
that person instead of through the room.

A Zig program dials a peer over encrypted TCP, fetches a capability it published,
and calls it:

```zig
var hint_storage: [1]glowcap.ocapn.model.HintEntry = undefined;
const peer_key = "<64 lowercase hex characters from the server>";
const partner = glowcap.net.tcp.noise.locator(
    peer_key,
    "127.0.0.1:9099",
    &hint_storage,
);
const session = try glowcap.driver.connect(
    allocator,
    io,
    .{ .symbols = &.{.noise_tcp} },
    partner,
    .ephemeral,
    .{ .handshake = .{ .ms = 10_000 }, .settle = .{ .ms = 10_000 } },
);
defer session.deinit();
const greeter = try session.fetch("greeter"); // a capability this peer published
var arena = std.heap.ArenaAllocator.init(allocator);
defer arena.deinit();
const settled = try session.call(greeter, "greet", &.{.{ .string = "world" }}, arena.allocator());
```

[Start building]($link.page('start')) shows the listening side, the other
languages, and the other transports.

Pin a commit, and expect the API to move.
[What you can depend on]($link.page('stability')) separates checked behavior
from areas that still need outside testing.

The protocol core does no I/O of its own. It is a synchronous state machine you
feed bytes into and drain bytes out of, so sockets, threads, and scheduling stay
in your program. Peers can meet over encrypted discovery
([iroh]($link.page('transports/iroh'))), sealed TCP
([Noise]($link.page('transports/noise'))), a browser DataChannel
([WebRTC]($link.page('transports/webrtc'))), or Tor
([onion]($link.page('transports/onion'))), among other paths.
[Choosing a transport]($link.page('transports/choosing')) is the decision table.

Use the library as a Zig package, a plain C ABI, or a binding (Rust,
TypeScript, Python, Racket). In the browser, the TypeScript binding calls the
core compiled to WebAssembly. The [CapTP
CLI]($link.page('cli')) provides shell commands, a REPL, and MCP tools. `zig build`
puts the `glowcap` binary in `zig-out/bin`. [Choose an
API]($link.page('surfaces')) compares all of these entry points.
